 f(n) = n \\) for all \\( n \\), the function is strictly increasing and multiplicative. For composite numbers that can be factored into coprime parts, the multiplicative property holds. For prime powers, the strictly increasing property ensures \\( f(p^k) = p^k \\).\n\nBy considering all these points and verifying through induction and the multiplicative property, we conclude that the only function satisfying all conditions is the identity function.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(n) = n}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Given Conditions and Initial Observations:**\n\n   We are given a function \\( f: \\mathbb{N} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{N} \\) that is strictly increasing. The conditions are:\n   - \\( f(2) = 2 \\)\n   - For all coprime \\( m \\) and \\( n \\), \\( f(mn) = f(m) f(n) \\).\n\n   Because \\( f \\) is strictly increasing, and assuming natural numbers are mapped to themselves as a base case, we propose \\( f(0) = 0 \\) and \\( f(1) = 1 \\). However, strictly increasing starting from 0 assumes \\( f(1) > 0 \\). For simplicity, we focus on \\( n \\geq 1 \\).\n\n2. **Determining the value of \\( f(3) \\):**\n   \n   Assume \\( f(3) = 3 + k \\) where \\( k \\geq 0 \\).\n\n   - We use \\( n = 6 \\):\n     \\[\n     f(6) = f(2 \\cdot 3) = f(2) \\cdot f(3) = 2 \\cdot (3 + k) = 6 + 2k.\n     \\]\n\n   - Considering bounds:\n     \\[\n     f(5) \\leq 5 + k \\quad \\text{since \\( f \\) is strictly increasing}.\n     \\]\n\n   - Use \\( n = 10 \\):\n     \\[\n     f(10) = f(2 \\cdot 5) = f(2) \\cdot f(5) = 2 \\cdot f(5).\n     \\]\n     Given \\( f(5) \\leq 5 + k \\):\n     \\[\n     f(10) \\leq 2 \\cdot (5 + k) = 10 + 2k.\n     \\]\n\n   - Use \\( n = 9 \\):\n     \\[\n     f(9) = f(3 \\cdot 3) = f(3)^2 = (3 + k)^2 = 9 + 6k + k^2.\n     \\]\n     Given \\( f(9) \\leq 4k + 9 \\):\n     \\[\n     9 + 6k + k^2 \\leq 4k + 9.\n     \\]\n     Simplifying, we get:\n     \\[\n     k^2 + 2k \\leq 0 \\Rightarrow k(k + 2) \\leq 0.\n     \\]\n     Since \\( k \\geq 0 \\):\n     \\[\n     k = 0.\n     \\]\n     Therefore, \\( f(3) = 3 \\).\n\n3. **Recurrence Proof \\( f(k) = k \\):**\n\n   We use induction to show \\( f(k) = k \\) for \\( k \\geq 1 \\).\n\n   - **Base Case:**\n     For \\( n = 1 \\), we have verified that \\( f(1) = 1, f(2) = 2 \\), and \\( f(3) = 3 \\).\n\n   - **Inductive Step:**\n     Assume \\( f(k) = k \\) for all \\( k \\leq 2^n + 1 \\).\n\n     For \\( n+1 \\):\n     \\[\n     f(2^{n+1} + 2) = f(2 \\cdot (2^n + 1)) = f(2) \\cdot f(2^n + 1) = 2 \\cdot (2^n + 1) = 2^{n+1} + 2.\n     \\]\n     Hence, for \\( k \\leq 2^{n+1} + 1 \\), \\( f(k) = k \\).\n\n     Since \\( f \\) is strictly increasing and covers all numbers up to \\( 2^{n+1} + 2 \\), we conclude \\( f(k) = k \\) for all \\( k \\leq 2^{n+2} + 2 \\).\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n\n   By the principle of induction, \\( f(k) = k \\) for all \\( k \\in \\mathbb{N} \\). Therefore, the only function satisfying the given conditions is:\n   \\[\n   f(k) = k.\n   \\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(k) = k}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Aanand divides the numbers $1, 2, 3, \\cdots, 2n-1, 2n$ into two groups, each consisting of $n$ numbers. Let $a_{1}<a_{2}<\\cdots<a_{n}$ be the first group of numbers written in ascending order and $b_{1}>b_{2}>\\cdots>b_{n}$ be the same first group of numbers written in descending order. Prove that $\\left|a_{1}-b_{1}\\right|+\\left|a_{2}-b_{2}\\right|+\\cdots+\\left|a_{n}-b_{n}\\right|=n^{2}$.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that when you split the numbers from 1 to 2n into two groups each of size n, and then take one group, sort it in ascending order as a\u2081 < a\u2082 < ... < a\u2099, and then also sort the same group in descending order as b\u2081 > b\u2082 > ... > b\u2099, the sum of the absolute differences |a\u2081 - b\u2081| + |a\u2082 - b\u2082| + ... + |a\u2099 - b\u2099| equals n\u00b2. Hmm, interesting. Let me try to unpack this step by step.\n\nFirst, l