oints, the area is zero, so they are not maxima.\n\nTherefore, the maximum area is 3\u221a3/8.\n\nWait, but let me check another approach to be thorough. Maybe using coordinates.\n\nWe have P = (0,0), Y = (cos \u03b8, 0), X = (cos \u03b8, 1 + sin \u03b8). So the coordinates of the triangle are (0,0), (cos \u03b8,0), (cos \u03b8,1 + sin \u03b8). The area can also be calculated as 1/2 * base * height. The base is PY, which is |cos \u03b8 - 0| = |cos \u03b8|. The height is the vertical distance from X to the line PY. Since PY is on the x-axis, the height is just the y-coordinate of X, which is 1 + sin \u03b8. So area is 1/2 * |cos \u03b8| * (1 + sin \u03b8), which is the same as before.\n\nAlternatively, using vectors. The vectors from P to X is (cos \u03b8, 1 + sin \u03b8), and from P to Y is (cos \u03b8, 0). The area is 1/2 |PX \u00d7 PY|. The cross product in 2D is scalar magnitude |ax by - ay bx|. So PX = (cos \u03b8, 1 + sin \u03b8), PY = (cos \u03b8, 0). The cross product is cos \u03b8 * 0 - (1 + sin \u03b8) * cos \u03b8 = - (1 + sin \u03b8) cos \u03b8. The absolute value is | - (1 + sin \u03b8) cos \u03b8 | = |cos \u03b8| (1 + sin \u03b8). So area is 1/2 |cos \u03b8| (1 + sin \u03b8). Same as before.\n\nThus, confirming the area expression. So the problem reduces to maximizing |cos \u03b8| (1 + sin \u03b8) over \u03b8, then dividing by 2.\n\nSince |cos \u03b8| (1 + sin \u03b8) is non-negative, we can consider squaring it to make differentiation easier, but perhaps not necessary. Alternatively, note that since both |cos \u03b8| and (1 + sin \u03b8) are non-negative, the maximum occurs at the same \u03b8 where the product is maximum.\n\nBut we already did the differentiation and found critical points at \u03b8 = \u03c0/6 and 5\u03c0/6, both giving 3\u221a3/8 as the maximum area.\n\nWait, but let's check \u03b8 where sin \u03b8 = -1. If sin \u03b8 = -1, then \u03b8 = 3\u03c0/2, which is point P, giving area zero. So that's a minimum.\n\nTherefore, the maximum area 