s on Circle 1. Similarly, CD is a chord of Circle 2 because C is on Circle 2 and D is on Circle 2. So BD is part of Circle 1, and CD is part of Circle 2.\n\nBut since B is outside Circle 2 (since AB is tangent to Circle 2 at A, and B is on Circle 1), the power of B with respect to Circle 2 is AB^2. The power can also be calculated as the product of the distances from B to the points where any secant line from B intersects Circle 2. If we take the secant BD, which passes through D (which is on both circles), then BD would intersect Circle 2 at D and another point. Wait, but BD is from B to D, which is on Circle 1 and Circle 2. But is BD passing through another point of Circle 2? If B is on Circle 1 and D is on both circles, then BD is a chord of Circle 1, but does BD intersect Circle 2 again? Since D is already on Circle 2, BD is a line from B (on Circle 1) to D (on both circles). Therefore, BD intersects Circle 2 only at D. Wait, but if BD is only intersecting Circle 2 at D, then the power of B with respect to Circle 2 would be BD * 0 (since there's only one intersection point), which doesn't make sense. Wait, maybe BD is tangent to Circle 2 at D? But that's not necessarily the case. Hmm, perhaps I need a different approach.\n\nAlternatively, consider the power of point B with respect to Circle 2. Since AB is tangent to Circle 2 at A, the power of B is AB\u00b2. Alternatively, if we take another secant line from B intersecting Circle 2, but BD is a line from B to D, which is on Circle 2. If BD intersects Circle 2 only at D, then BD is tangent to Circle 2 at D. But we don't know that. So unless BD is tangent, the power formula won't apply directly. Wait, perhaps BD is not tangent. Then, BD is a secant that intersects Circle 2 at D and another point. But BD connects B to D. If B is outside Circle 2, then BD would intersect Circle 2 at two points: D and another point. Wait, but D is already a point on both circles. If B is outside Circle 2, then BD would pass through D