*\n\n1. The unit digit of \\( 3^{2003} \\):\n   - The cycle for 3 is [3, 9, 7, 1]. \\( 2003 \\mod 4 = 3 \\), so the unit digit is 7.\n2. The unit digit of \\( 5^{2002} \\):\n   - Any power of 5 ends in 5.\n3. The unit digit of \\( 7^{2001} \\):\n   - The cycle for 7 is [7, 9, 3, 1]. \\( 2001 \\mod 4 = 1 \\), so the unit digit is 7.\n4. Multiplying these unit digits: \\( 7 \\times 5 \\times 7 = 245 \\). The unit digit is 5.\n\nThus, \\( P = \\boxed{5} \\).\n\n**Problem I1.2: Find the number of integral solutions for \\( \\left(x^{2}-x-1\\right)^{x+4}=1 \\).**\n\n1. **Case 1: \\( x^2 - x - 1 = 1 \\)**\n   - Solutions: \\( x = 2 \\) and \\( x = -1 \\).\n2. **Case 2: \\( x^2 - x - 1 = -1 \\)**\n   - Solutions: \\( x = 0 \\) (valid, exponent even) and \\( x = 1 \\) (invalid, exponent odd).\n3. **Case 3: Exponent \\( x + 4 = 0 \\)**\n   - Solution: \\( x = -4 \\).\n\nTotal solutions: \\( x = -4, -1, 0, 2 \\). Thus, \\( Q = \\boxed{4} \\).\n\n**Problem I1.3: Find the minimum value of \\( \\frac{1}{x^4} + \\frac{1}{4y^4} \\) given \\( xy = 1 \\).**\n\n1. Substitute \\( y = \\frac{1}{x} \\): \\( \\frac{1}{x^4} + \\frac{x^4}{4} \\).\n2. Let \\( t = x^4 \\): Minimize \\( \\frac{1}{t} + \\frac{t}{4} \\).\n3. Using AM-GM inequality: Minimum value is 1 when \\( t = 2 \\).\n\nThus, \\( R = \\boxed{1} \\).\n\n**Problem I1.4: Find the maximum number of distinct positive integers summing to 2003.**\n\n1. The largest \\( k \\) such that \\( \\sum_{i=1}^k i \\leq 2003 \\) is \\( k = 62 \\) (sum = 1953).\n2. Adjust the last term: Replace 62 with 112 (sum difference 50).\n\nThus, the maximum number of terms is 62. Therefore, \\( S = \\boxed{62} \\).\n\n### Final Answers\n\nI1.1: \\boxed{5}\n\nI1.2: \\boxed{4}\n\nI1.3: \\boxed{1}\n\nI1.4: \\boxed{62}", "ground_truth_solution": "\n**1.1 Finding the unit digit $P$ of $3^{2003} \\times 5^{2002} \\times 7^{2001}$**\n\n1. To find the unit digit of a product, observe the unit digits of the individual terms and their cyclic patterns.\n2. Note that $3^{2003}$ has unit digit cyclic pattern (3, 9, 7, 1):\n   \\[\n   3^1 = 3, \\quad 3^2 = 9, \\quad 3^3 = 27, \\quad 3^4 = 81 \\quad (\\text{repeats every 4 terms})\n   \\]\n3. Determine the power cycle position for $3^{2003}$:\n   \\[\n   2003 \\mod 4 = 3 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\text{unit digit of } 3^{2003} \\text{ is } 7\n   \\]\n4. The unit digit of $5^{2002}$ is always 5:\n   \\[\n   5^1 = 5, \\quad 5^2 = 25, \\quad 5^3 = 125, \\quad \\text{(always ends in 5)}\n   \\]\n5. The unit digit of $7^{2001}$ has cyclic pattern (7, 9, 3, 1):\n   \\[\n   7^1 = 7, \\quad 7^2 = 49, \\quad 7^3 = 343, \\quad 7^4 = 2401 \\quad (\\text{repeats every 4 terms})\n   \\]\n6. Determine the power cycle position for $7^{2001}$:\n   \\[\n   2001 \\mod 4 = 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\text{unit digit of } 7^{2001} \\text{ is } 7\n   \\]\n7. Calculate the unit digit of the product:\n   \\[\n   (3^{2003} \\times 5^{2002} \\times 7^{2001}) \\mod 10 = (7 \\times 5 \\times 7) \\mod 10\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = (35 \\times 7) \\mod 10 = 5 \\times 7 = 35 \\Rightarrow 5\n   \\]\n8. The unit digit $P$ is: \n   \\[\n   \\boxed{5}\n   \\]\n\n**1.2 Finding the value of $Q$**\n\n1. Given the equation:\n   \\[\n   (x^2 - x - 1)^{x + P - 1} = 1\n   \\]\n   We need to find integral solutions for $x$ with $P = 5$:\n   \\[\n   (x^2 - x - 1)^{x + 4} = 1\n   \\]\n\n2. Cases analysis for $(x^2 - x - 1)^{x + 4} = 1$:\n\n   - **Case 1: $(x^2 - x - 1) = 1$**\n     \\[\n     x^2 - x - 2 = 0 \n     \\]\n     \\[\n     (x - 2)(x + 1) = 0\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     x = 2, -1\n     \\]\n\n   - **Case 2: $x + 4 = 0$**\n     \\[\n     x = -4\n     \\]\n\n   - **Case 3: $(x^2 - x - 1) = -1$ and $x + 4$ is even**\n     - Solve $x^2 - x = 0$:\n     \\[\n     x(x - 1) = 0 \\Rightarrow x = 0, 1\n     \\]\n     - Since $x + 4$ needs to be even:\n       \\[\n       \\text{Only } x = 0 \\, (\\text{integral value})\n       \\]\n\n3. Collecting all integer solutions:\n   \\[\n   x = 2, -1, -4, 0\n   \\]\n\n4. Therefore, the number of integral solutions $Q$ is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{4}\n   \\]\n\n**1.3 Finding the minimum value $R$ of $\\frac{1}{x^4} + \\frac{1}{Qy^4}$**\n\n1. Given $xy = 1$, and $Q = 4$:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{x^4} + \\frac{1}{4y^4}\n   \\]\n   Using $AM \\geq GM$:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\frac{1}{x^4} + \\frac{1}{4y^4}}{2} \\geq \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{x^4} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4y^4}}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\frac{1}{x^4} + \\frac{1}{4y^4}}{2} \\geq \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4x^4 y^4}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4 \\cdot 1^4}} = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. Therefore, the minimum value:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{x^4} + \\frac{1}{4y^4} \\geq 2 \\times \\frac{1}{2} = 1\n   \\]\n\n3. Hence, the minimum value $R$ is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{1}\n   \\]\n\n**1.4 Finding the maximum possible value $S$**\n\n1. Given $x_R + x_{R+1} +...+ x_K = 2003$ with $K - R + 1$ distinct positive integers. Maximizing $K$:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{K-1} i + x_K = 2003\n   \\]\n2. Converting to sum of first $K-1$ natural numbers:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(K-1)K}{2} + x_K = 2003\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x_K \\geq K\n   \\]\n3. So:\n   \\[\n   2003 \\geq \\frac{(K-1)K}{2} + K\n   \\]\n4. Multiply all sides by 2:\n   \\[\n   4006 \\geq K^2 + K\n   \\]\n5. Solving quadratic inequality:\n   \\[\n   K^2 + K - 4006 \\leq 0\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   K = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 + 4 \\times 4006}}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{16025}}{2}\n   \\]\n6. Approximate:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{16025} \\approx 127\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   K = \\frac{-1 + 127}{2} = 63\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{4006} \\approx 63\n   \\]\n7. Verification with:\n   \\[\n   (K - 1) +  \\sum_{i=1}^{K-1} = \\text{ satisfies}\n   \\]\n\n   - Maximize $K = 62$ since $62-1953 = 50$.\n   \n8. Thus the maximum possible value $S$ is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{62}\n   \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "The product of natural numbers \\( m \\) and \\( n \\) is divisible by their sum. Prove that \\( m + n \\leq n^2 \\).", "dee