ective is to distribute the cement evenly among all warehouses. \n\n1. **Initial Hypothesis**: Assume that all cement is located at a single warehouse \\( S \\).\n\n2. **Observation**: In order to distribute the cement, some amount of cement must be transported to each of the other \\( 2010 \\) warehouses. Therefore, we need at least 2010 trips to ensure every other warehouse receives some cement from \\( S \\).\n\n3. **Inductive Proof**: We will use mathematical induction to prove that for \\( n \\) warehouses, at least \\( n-1 \\) trips are required.\n\n    - **Base Case** (\\( n = 1 \\)): When there is only one warehouse, no trips are needed as there are no other warehouses to distribute to. Hence, 0 trips are sufficient, which is \\( 1 - 1 = 0 \\).\n\n    - **Inductive Step**: Assume that for \\( k \\) warehouses, \\( k-1 \\) trips are sufficient. Now consider \\( k+1 \\) warehouses. Since every warehouse is connected, there exists a route connecting all warehouses. \n\n        - Identify a warehouse \\( A \\) that appears last in one of these routes.\n        - If we remove warehouse \\( A \\) and all roads leading to it, the remaining \\( k \\) warehouses are still connected.\n        - By the inductive hypothesis, it requires \\( k-1 \\) trips to achieve the required distribution among the \\( k \\) warehouses.\n\n4. **Inductive Application for \\( n \\) Warehouses**:\n    - If \\( y_n \\leq x_n \\), transfer \\( x_n - y_n \\) kg of cement from warehouse \\( n \\) to any connected warehouse and ignore \\( A \\) and its roads. The hypothesis ensures that for the remaining \\( n-1 \\) warehouses, \\( n-2 \\) trips are sufficient. Therefore, with the additional trip made, the total becomes \\( (n-2) + 1 = n-1 \\).\n    - If \\( y_n > x_n \\), we can reverse the distribution process: pretend we are redistributing cement to achieve the original starting points of \\( x_i \\) from \\( y_i \\). Going back requires \\( n-1 \\) trips as proved, implying the forward di