os b cos c <0. So one of them is negative. If we have b obtuse (cos b <0), then c could be acute (cos c >0) or obtuse (cos c <0). If c is obtuse, then cos c <0, so from inequality 3:\n\ncos c > cos a cos b\n\nBut cos c is negative, and cos a cos b is positive (since both a and b are obtuse, cos a <0, cos b <0, so product positive). So cos c (negative) > positive number. Contradiction. Therefore, if a and b are obtuse, then we have a contradiction via inequality 3. Alternatively, if a is obtuse, and only one of b or c is obtuse, say b is obtuse and c is acute. Then, inequality 3:\n\ncos c > cos a cos b\n\nBut cos c is positive (c is acute), and cos a cos b is positive (a and b obtuse). So inequality 3 becomes positive > positive. Let's plug in numbers. Suppose a and b are 120 degrees (cos 120\u00b0 = -0.5). Let cos a = cos b = -0.5. Then inequality 3:\n\ncos c > (-0.5)(-0.5) = 0.25\n\nSo cos c > 0.25. Therefore, c < arccos(0.25) \u2248 75.5 degrees. So c is acute. Then, from inequality 1:\n\ncos a > cos b cos c\n\nLeft-hand side: cos a = -0.5\n\nRight-hand side: cos b cos c = (-0.5)(cos c) > (-0.5)(1) = -0.5\n\nBut since cos c >0.25, cos b cos c = (-0.5)(cos c) < (-0.5)(0.25) = -0.125\n\nSo inequality 1: -0.5 > -0.125? That's not true. -0.5 is not greater than -0.125. So this is a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, if a and b are obtuse, then inequality 1 fails. Similarly, if only a is obtuse and b is acute, but then from inequality 1, cos a > cos b cos c. Since cos a is negative, cos b cos c must be less than cos a. But cos b is positive (b acute), cos c is positive (if c is acute), so their product is positive, which cannot be less than negative cos a. Contradiction. If c is obtuse instead, then cos c is negative, so cos b cos c is negative (since cos b positive, cos c negative). Then, cos a > negative number. But cos a is negative, so this would require that cos a is greater than some negative number. For example, if cos a = -0.5, and cos b cos c = -0.6, then -0.5 > -0.