egative, cos a cos c \u22650. Therefore, cos a cos c is either non-positive or non-negative. But cos b is negative, so the inequality cos b > cos a cos c would be:\n\nNegative > [non-positive or non-negative]\n\nIf cos a cos c is non-positive (i.e., cos c \u22650), then cos b > non-positive. Since cos b is negative, this is possible only if cos a cos c is less than cos b. However, cos a cos c is non-positive and cos b is negative. For example, if cos a cos c is -0.1 and cos b is -0.2, then -0.2 > -0.1 is false. Wait, so if cos a cos c is greater than cos b, which is negative, then the inequality fails. Alternatively, if cos a cos c is positive (i.e., cos c <0), then cos a cos c is positive (since cos a \u22640 and cos c <0). Then, the inequality is cos b (negative) > positive, which is impossible. Hence, in either case, there is a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, our assumption that cos a \u22640 leads to a contradiction. Therefore, cos a must be positive. Similarly, cos b and cos c must be positive. Therefore, all plane angles a, b, c are acute.\n\nSo, this seems to confirm the original statement. If all dihedral angles are acute, then all plane angles must be acute.\n\nAnother way to think about it is by considering the dual problem. If a plane angle were obtuse, then the corresponding dihedral angle would have to be obtuse as well. But since all dihedral angles are given as acute, this can't happen. Therefore, all plane angles must be acute.\n\nAlternatively, visualizing a trihedral angle with all dihedral angles acute. If one of the faces had an obtuse plane angle, the \"opening\" between the two edges forming that angle would be wider, possibly creating a larger dihedral angle. But since all dihedral angles are constrained to be acute, the plane angles can't afford to be obtuse.\n\nIn summary, through applying the spherical law of cosines to the spherical triangle formed by the trihedral angle, we derived that each plane angle's cosine must be positive given that all dihedral angles are acute. This directly leads to the conclusion that all plane angles are acute. Additionally, by assuming the contrary and reaching contradictions through the inequalities derived from the spherical trigonometry relations, we reinforced the validity of the proof.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that if all dihedral angles of a trihedral angle are acute, then all plane angles are also acute, we utilize principles from spherical trigonometry and analyze the relationships between dihedral and plane angles.\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Definitions:**\n   - A **trihedral angle** is formed by three intersecting planes, creating three edges and three face planes.\n   - **Dihedral angles** are the angles between pairs of face planes.\n   - **Plane angles** are the angles between pairs of edges on each face.\n\n2. **Spherical Triangle Representation:**\n   - Consider a unit sphere centered at the trihedral angle's vertex. The intersection of the trihedral angle with the sphere forms a **spherical triangle**.\n   - The **sides** of this spherical triangle correspond to the **plane angles** \\(a, b, c\\) of the trihedral angle.\n   - The **angles** of the spherical triangle correspond to the **dihedral angles** \\(\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma\\) of the trihedral angle.\n\n3. **Spherical Law of Cosines:**\n   For a spherical triangle with sides \\(a, b, c\\) and opposite angles \\(\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma\\):\n   \\[\n   \\cos a = \\cos b \\cos c + \\sin b \\sin c \\cos \\alpha\n   \\]\n   Similar equations apply for \\(\\cos b\\) and \\(\\cos c\\).\n\n4. **Given Condition:**\n   All dihedral angles \\(\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma\\) are acute (\\(\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma < 90^\\circ\\)), so \\(\\cos \\alpha, \\cos \\beta, \\cos \\gamma > 0\\).\n\n5. **Analyzing Plane Angles:**\n   Using the spherical law of cosines for each plane angle:\n   \\[\n   \\cos a = \\cos b \\cos c + \\sin b \\sin c \\cos \\alpha\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\cos \\alpha > 0\\), \\(