n\nSolving this, we get roots \\(r = 1\\) and \\(r = -\\frac{1}{2}\\). The general solution to the homogeneous equation is:\n\n\\[ E_n^h = A + B\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n \\]\n\nFor a particular solution, we try \\(E_n^p = \\frac{2}{3}n\\), which satisfies the nonhomogeneous equation. Combining these, the general solution is:\n\n\\[ E_n = \\frac{2}{3}n + A + B\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n \\]\n\nUsing the boundary conditions \\(E_0 = 0\\) and \\(E_1 = 1\\):\n\nFor \\(n = 0\\):\n\\[ 0 = A + B \\]\n\nFor \\(n = 1\\):\n\\[ 1 = \\frac{2}{3} + A - \\frac{B}{2} \\]\n\nSolving these, we find \\(A = \\frac{2}{9}\\) and \\(B = -\\frac{2}{9}\\). Thus, the solution is:\n\n\\[ E_n = \\frac{2}{3}n + \\frac{2}{9} - \\frac{2}{9}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n \\]\n\nAs \\(n \\to \\infty\\), the term \\(-\\frac{2}{9}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n\\) decays to zero, leading to:\n\n\\[ E_n \\approx \\frac{2}{3}n + \\frac{2}{9} \\]\n\nThus, the constants \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are \\(\\frac{2}{3}\\) and \\(\\frac{2}{9}\\), respectively. The pair \\((a, b)\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left( \\dfrac{2}{3}, \\dfrac{2}{9} \\right)}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. We start by examining the recurrence relation for \\(E_n\\). According to the problem, if we toss a coin from the number \\(n\\), then:\n\n    - If heads appear, the next step will be \\(n-1\\).\n    - If tails appear, the next step will be \\(n-2\\).\n    \n    Therefore, the expected value \\(E_n\\) can be expressed as:\n    \\[\n    E_n = \\frac{1}{2}(E_{n-1} + 1) + \\frac{1}{2}(E_{n-2} + 1)\n    \\]\n\n2. Simplifying this recurrence relation:\n    \\[\n    E_n = \\frac{1}{2} E_{n-1} + \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2} E_{n-2} + \\frac{1}{2} \n    \\]\n    \\[\n    E_n = 1 + \\frac{1}{2} (E_{n-1} + E_{n-2}) \\quad \\text{(for } n \\geqslant 2\\text{)}\n    \\]\n\n3. Define \\(F_n\\) as:\n    \\[\n    F_n = E_n - \\frac{2}{3}n\n    \\]\n\n4. Substitute \\(E_n\\) into the recurrence relation in terms of \\(F_n\\):\n    \\[\n    F_n = E_n - \\frac{2}{3}n = 1 + \\frac{1}{2}(E_{n-1} - \\frac{2}{3}(n-1) + E_{n-2} - \\frac{2}{3}(n-2))\n    \\]\n    Since:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{2}{3}n = \\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{1}{3}n + \\frac{1}{3}n\n    \\]\n\n5. We simplify the equation:\n    \\[\n    F_n = 1 + \\frac{1}{2}\\left(F_{n-1} + F_{n-2}\\right) + 0\n    \\]\n    Thus, our simplified recurrence relation for \\(F_n\\) becomes:\n    \\[\n    F_n = \\frac{1}{2}(F_{n-1} + F_{n-2})\n    \\]\n\n6. The characteristic equation for this recurrence is:\n    \\[\n    x^2 - \\frac{1}{2}x - \\frac{1}{2}  = 0\n    \\]\n\n7. Solving the characteristic equation:\n    \\[\n    x = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{1+2}}{2} = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{3}}{2}\n    \\]\n    Hence, the roots are \\(1\\) and \\(-\\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\n8. Therefore, the general solution for \\(F_n\\) can be written as:\n    \\[\n    F_n = A + B \\left( -\\frac{1}{2} \\right)^n\n    \\]\n    where \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are constants determined by initial conditions.\n\n9. Using the initial conditions:\n    - For \\(n = 0\\): \\( F_0 = E_0 - \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot 0 = 0 \\Rightarrow A + B = 0\\)\n    - For \\(n = 1\\): \\( F_1 = E_1 - \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot 1 = 1 - \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{1}{3} \\Rightarrow A - \\frac{1}{2}B = \\frac{1}{3} \\)\n\n10. Solving the system of equations:\n    \\[\n    A + B = 0\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    A - \\frac{1}{2}B = \\frac{1}{3}\n    \\]\n    Adding both equations:\n    \\[\n    \\left(A + B\\right) + \\left(A - \\frac{ 1}{2} B\\right) = \\frac{1}{3}\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    2A + \\frac{1}{2} B = \\frac{2}{9}\n    \\]\n\n11. We find that:\n    \\[\n    A = \\frac{2}{9} \\quad B = -\\frac{2}{9}\n    \\]\n\n12. Substituting back in for \\(F_n\\):\n    \\[\n    F_n = \\frac{2}{9} - \\frac{2}{9} \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n\n    \\]\n\n13. Thus \\(E_n\\) is found as:\n    \\[\n    E_n = \\frac{2}{3}n + F_n = \\frac{2}{3}n + \\frac{2}{9} - \\frac{2}{9}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n\n    \\]\n\n14. Assuming the limit \\(\\lim _{n \\rightarrow \\infty}\\left(E_{n}-a n-b\\right)=0\\), for \\( (a, b) \\):\n    \\[\n    a = \\frac{2}{3}, \\quad b = \\frac{2}{9}\n    \\]\n   \n15. Thus:\n    \\[\n    (a, b)=\\left(\\frac{2}{3}, \\frac{2}{9}\\right)\n    \\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}, \\frac{2}{9}\\right)}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let $\\xi$ be a non-negative random variable and $\\mathscr{G}$ a $\\sigma$-subalgebra of the $\\sigma$-algebra $\\mathscr{F}$. Show that $\\mathrm{E}[\\xi \\mid \\mathscr{G}]<\\infty$ almost surely if and only if the measure $\\mathrm{Q}$, defined on sets $A \\in \\mathscr{G}$ by $\\mathrm{Q}(A)=\\int_{A} \\xi d \\mathrm{P}$, is $\\sigma$-finite.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to work through this problem step by step. The problem is about showing that the conditional expectation E[\u03be | \ud835\udca2] is finite almost surely if and only if the measure Q defined by Q(A) = \u222b_A \u03be dP for A \u2208 \ud835\udca2 is \u03c3-finite. \n\nFirst, I need to recall some definitions. A \u03c3-algebra \ud835\udca2 is a sub-\u03c3-algebra of \ud835\udca2, so \ud835\udca2 contains some events, and Q is a measure on \ud835\udca2 defined by integrating \u03be over sets in \ud835\udca2. The question is linking the finiteness of the conditional expectation to the \u03c3-finiteness of Q. \n\nLet me start by understanding both directions. The \"if and only if\" means I have to prove two implications: \n\n1. If E[\u03be | \ud835\udca2] is finite a.s., then Q is \u03c3-finite.\n2. If Q is \u03c3-finite, then E[\u03be | \ud835\udca2] is finite a.s.\n\nI need to tackle each direction separately. Let's start with the first one. \n\n**Forward Direction (E[\u03be | \ud835\udca2] < \u221e a.s. \u21d2 Q is \u03c3-finite):**\n\nAssuming that E[\u03be | \ud835\udca2] is finite almost surely. Let me denote \u03b7 = E[\u03be | \ud835\udca2], so \u03b7 is \ud835\udca2-measurable and \u03b7 < \u221e a.s. Since \u03b7