 \\{\\eta = \\infty\\}\\) has \\(\\mathrm{P}(N) = 0\\).\n2. **Construct sets \\(G_n = \\{\\eta \\leq n\\}\\)**: These sets are in \\(\\mathscr{G}\\) and cover \\(N^c\\) (the complement of \\(N\\)), which is almost the entire space \\(\\Omega\\).\n3. **Check \\(\\mathrm{Q}\\)-measure of \\(G_n\\)**: For each \\(G_n\\), \\(\\mathrm{Q}(G_n) = \\int_{G_n} \\xi \\, d\\mathrm{P} = \\int_{G_n} \\eta \\, d\\mathrm{P}\\). Since \\(\\eta \\leq n\\) on \\(G_n\\), \\(\\mathrm{Q}(G_n) \\leq n \\cdot \\mathrm{P}(G_n) \\leq n < \\infty\\).\n4. **Cover the space**: The sets \\(G_n\\) along with \\(N\\) (which has \\(\\mathrm{Q}(N) = 0\\)) cover \\(\\Omega\\). Thus, \\(\\mathrm{Q}\\) is \\(\\sigma\\)-finite.\n\n### Reverse Direction (\\(\\mathrm{Q}\\) is \\(\\sigma\\)-finite \\(\\Rightarrow\\) \\(\\mathrm{E}[\\xi \\mid \\mathscr{G}] < \\infty\\) a.s.)\n\n1. **Assume \\(\\mathrm{Q}\\) is \\(\\sigma\\)-finite**: There exist sets \\(A_n \\in \\mathscr{G}\\) such that \\(\\Omega = \\bigcup_{n=1}^\\infty A_n\\) and \\(\\mathrm{Q}(A_n) < \\infty\\).\n2. **Suppose \\(\\eta = \\infty\\) on a set \\(B \\in \\mathscr{G}\\)**: If \\(\\mathrm{P}(B) > 0\\), then \\(\\mathrm{Q}(B) = \\int_B \\eta \\, d\\mathrm{P} = \\infty\\). However, \\(B\\) can be covered by \\(A_n\\), leading to \\(\\mathrm{Q}(B) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\mathrm{Q}(B \\cap A_n)\\). Each \\(\\mathrm{Q}(B \\cap A_n) \\leq \\mathrm{Q}(A_n) < \\infty\\), contradicting \\(\\mathrm{Q}(B) = \\infty\\).\n3. **Conclusion**: No such set \\(B\\) exists, so \\(\\eta\\) must be finite almost surely.\n\n### Final Answer\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathrm{E}[\\xi \\mid \\mathscr{G}]<\\infty \\text{ a.s.} \\iff Q \\text{ is } \\sigma\\text{-finite}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nWe are given a non-negative random variable \\(\\xi\\) and a \\(\\sigma\\)-subalgebra \\(\\mathscr{G}\\) of a \\(\\sigma\\)-algebra \\(\\mathscr{F}\\). We aim to demonstrate that \\(\\mathrm{E}[\\xi \\mid \\mathscr{G}] < \\infty\\) almost surely if and only if the measure \\(