tes of C and D, then using vectors to compute the dot products, which simplified to zero using the relationship c\u00b2 = a\u00b2 - b\u00b2. The product y_C y_D simplified to b\u00b2, which was crucial in cancelling out the remaining terms.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{\\angle CMD = \\angle CND = 90^\\circ}", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that \\(\\angle CMD = \\angle CND = 90^\\circ\\) for the given ellipse \\(\\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\\) with vertices \\(A(-a, 0)\\) and \\(B(a, 0)\\), and foci \\(N(-c, 0)\\) and \\(M(c, 0)\\) where \\(c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}\\):\n\n1. **Parametrize Point \\(P\\)**:\n   - Let \\(P\\) be \\((a \\cos \\theta, b \\sin \\theta)\\) on the ellipse.\n   - The tangent line at \\(P\\) is \\(\\frac{x \\cos \\theta}{a} + \\frac{y \\sin \\theta}{b} = 1\\).\n\n2. **Find Coordinates of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\)**:\n   - **Point \\(C\\)**: Intersection of the tangent at \\(P\\) with the vertical tangent \\(x = -a\\) at \\(A\\):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{-a \\cos \\theta}{a} + \\frac{y \\sin \\theta}{b} = 1 \\implies y = \\frac{b(1 + \\cos \\theta)}{\\sin \\theta}\n     \\]\n     Thus, \\(C = \\left(-a, \\frac{b(1 + \\cos \\theta)}{\\sin \\theta}\\right)\\).\n   - **Point \\(D\\)**: Intersection of the tangent at \\(P\\) with the vertical tangent \\(x = a\\) at \\(B\\):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{a \\cos \\theta}{a} + \\frac{y \\sin \\theta}{b} = 1 \\implies y = \\frac{b(1 - \\cos \\theta)}{\\sin \\theta}\n     \\]\n     Thus, \\(D = \\left(a, \\frac{b(1 - \\cos \\theta)}{\\sin \\theta}\\right)\\).\n\n3. **Calculate Vectors**:\n   - **Vectors \\(MC\\) and \\(MD\\)**:\n     \\[\n     \\overrightarrow{MC} = \\left(-a - c, \\frac{b(1 + \\cos \\theta)}{\\sin \\theta}\\right)\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\overrightarrow{MD} = \\left(a - c, \\frac{b(1 - \\cos \\theta)}{\\sin \\theta}\\right)\n     \\]\n   - **Vectors \\(NC\\) and \\(ND\\)**:\n     \\[\n     \\overrightarrow{NC} = \\left(-a + c, \\frac{b(1 + \\cos \\theta)}{\\sin \\theta}\\right)\n     \\]