mation:** Any parallelogram (including a rhombus) can be transformed into a rectangle by cutting along a height (perpendicular to the base) and sliding the resulting triangle to the opposite side. This method requires only one cut, yielding two pieces.\n\n4. **Detailed Steps:**\n   - **Cut Along the Height:** Choose a base side of the rhombus. Cut from the top vertex (opposite the base) perpendicularly down to the base, creating a right triangle and a quadrilateral.\n   - **Rearrange the Pieces:** Slide the cut-off triangle horizontally along the base to align with the opposite side. This straightens the slant of the rhombus into a rectangle, maintaining the original base and height.\n\n5. **Verification:**\n   - **Area Preservation:** The area of the rectangle formed (\\( \\text{base} \\times \\text{height} \\)) matches the rhombus's area.\n   - **Right Angles Achieved:** The rearrangement ensures all angles become 90 degrees, satisfying the rectangle's definition.\n\n**Conclusion:** Yes, any rhombus can be dissected into no more than **two pieces** that form a rectangle. This is achieved by cutting along the height and rearranging the resulting pieces. \n\n**Answer:**  \n\\boxed{Yes}", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Identifying Special Cases**:\n   - If the rhombus is a square, it is already a rectangle, and no cutting or rearrangement is needed.\n   - Conclusion for this case: Cutting is not required.\n\n2. **General Case**:\n   - Consider rhombus $ABCD$, where $AB = BC = CD = DA$ but it is not a square.\n   - Suppose $\\angle B$ and $\\angle D$ are acute angles, and $\\angle A$ and $\\angle C$ are obtuse angles.\n\n3. **Constructing the Cut**:\n   - Draw a perpendicular from the obtuse angle vertex $B$ to the opposite side $AD$ at point $H$ forming triangle $\\triangle ABH$. This segment $BH$ is the height of the rhombus (altitude from vertex $B$ perpendicular to $AD$).\n   - Note that height $BH$ lies entirely within the rhombus, as $AH < AB = AD$.\n\n4. **Cutting the Rhombus**:\n   - Cut the rhombus along the height $BH$, thereby splitting the rhombus into two right-angled triangles, $\\triangle ABH$ and $\\triangle BCD$.\n\n5. **Rearranging the Pieces**:\n   - Take the triangle $\\triangle ABH$ and align it along the side $CD$ of the other triangle $\\triangle BCD$ such that vertex $H$ touches $C$ and $A$ aligns with $D$.\n\n6. **Forming the Rectangle**:\n   - After aligning, you will form a rectangle because:\n     - Side $AD$ of the original rhombus becomes the length of the rectangle, and these were originally adjacent sides of the rhombus.\n     - The height $BH$ now becomes the width of the rectangle which is equal on both the re-arranged triangles.\n\n### Conclusion:\nThus, by cutting the rhombus once along the height from an obtuse angle and rearranging the resulting pieces, we get a rectangle. Therefore, it is possible to cut a rhombus into two pieces and rearrange them to form a rectangle.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In each cell of an \\( n \\times n \\) table, there is a natural number. It turns out that for all \\( k \\) from 1 to \\( n \\), the sum of the numbers in the \\( k \\)-th column differs by one from the sum of the numbers in the \\( k \\)-th row. For which \\( n \\) is this possible?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to tackle this problem step by step. The question is about an \\( n \\times n \\) table where each cell contains a natural number. The condition given is that for every \\( k \\) from 1 to \\( n \\), the sum of the numbers in the \\( k \\)-th column differs by one from the sum of the numbers in the \\( k \\)-th row. We need to determine for which values of \\( n \\) this is possible.\n\nFirst, let's restate the problem to make sure I understand it correctly. We have a square matrix where each entry is a natural number (so positive integers). For each column \\( k \\), th