f CA):\n\n|CA| = sqrt((-0.1736)\u00b2 + (-0.9848)\u00b2) \u2248 sqrt(0.0301 + 0.9698) \u2248 sqrt(0.9999) \u2248 1.0\n\nTherefore, cos(theta) \u2248 0.8268 / (0.9543 * 1.0) \u2248 0.8268 / 0.9543 \u2248 0.866\n\nSo theta \u2248 arccos(0.866) \u2248 30 degrees.\n\nWait, arccos(0.866) is approximately 30 degrees, since cos(30\u00b0) \u2248 0.866. \n\nTherefore, angle ACD is approximately 30 degrees. \n\nHmm, that seems straightforward. But let me verify this with another method to ensure accuracy. \n\nAlternatively, using Law of Sines in triangle ACD. To do that, I need to know some side lengths. Let's compute the lengths of AC, CD, and AD. \n\nAC is given as 1 unit. \n\nAD: distance from A(0,0) to D(0.5, 0.088):\n\nAD = sqrt((0.5)^2 + (0.088)^2) \u2248 sqrt(0.25 + 0.0077) \u2248 sqrt(0.2577) \u2248 0.5077, which matches our earlier parameter t.\n\nCD: distance from C(0.1736, 0.9848) to D(0.5, 0.088):\n\nCD \u2248 sqrt((0.5 - 0.1736)^2 + (0.088 - 0.9848)^2) \u2248 sqrt(0.1065 + 0.8043) \u2248 sqrt(0.9108) \u2248 0.9543, which matches earlier |CD|.\n\nNow, in triangle ACD, sides:\n\nAC = 1\nAD \u2248 0.5077\nCD \u2248 0.9543\n\nWe can apply the Law of Cosines to find angle at C:\n\ncos(angle ACD) = (AC\u00b2 + CD\u00b2 - AD\u00b2) / (2 * AC * CD)\n\nPlugging in the values:\n\ncos(angle ACD) = (1\u00b2 + 0.9543\u00b2 - 0.5077\u00b2) / (2 * 1 * 0.9543)\n\u2248 (1 + 0.9107 - 0.2578) / (1.9086)\n\u2248 (1.9107 - 0.2578) / 1.9086\n\u2248 1.6529 / 1.9086 \u2248 0.866\n\nWhich gives angle ACD \u2248 arccos(0.866) \u2248 30\u00b0, confirming the earlier result. \n\nBut wait, in the original triangle, angle at C is 50 degrees. If angle ACD is 30 degrees, then angle BCD would be 50 - 30 = 20 degrees? Let me check that. \n\nBut point D is inside the triangle, so in triangle BCD, angle at C is angle BCD, which we just calculated as 20 degrees? Wait, maybe not. Let me see. \n\nAlternatively, maybe we can use other angle relationships. \n\nBut given that both calculations (vector do