 \\) are each \\( 50^\\circ \\). Point \\( D \\) is inside the triangle such that \\( \\angle DAB = \\angle DBA = 10^\\circ \\). We need to find the measure of \\( \\angle ACD \\).\n\n1. **Angle Calculation**:\n   - In \\( \\triangle ABC \\), since \\( AB = AC \\) and \\( \\angle BAC = 80^\\circ \\), the base angles are \\( \\angle ABC = \\angle ACB = 50^\\circ \\).\n   - Point \\( D \\) is such that \\( \\angle DAB = 10^\\circ \\) and \\( \\angle DBA = 10^\\circ \\). This makes \\( \\angle BAD = 10^\\circ \\) and \\( \\angle ABD = 10^\\circ \\).\n\n2. **Using Ceva's Theorem**:\n   - Applying the trigonometric form of Ceva's theorem for concurrent cevians \\( AD \\), \\( BD \\), and \\( CD \\):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{\\sin(\\angle BAD)}{\\sin(\\angle CAD)} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin(\\angle CBD)}{\\sin(\\angle ABD)} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin(\\angle ACD)}{\\sin(\\angle BCD)} = 1\n     \\]\n   - Substituting the known angles:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{\\sin(10^\\circ)}{\\sin(70^\\circ)} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin(40^\\circ)}{\\sin(10^\\circ)} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin(x)}{\\sin(50^\\circ - x)} = 1\n     \\]\n   - Simplifying:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{\\sin(40^\\circ)}{\\sin(70^\\circ)} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin(x)}{\\sin(50^\\circ - x)} = 1\n     \\]\n   - Solving for \\( x \\):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{\\sin(x)}{\\sin(50^\\circ - x)} = \\frac{\\sin(70^\\circ)}{\\sin(40^\\circ)}\n     \\]\n   - Testing \\( x = 30^\\circ \\):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{\\sin(30^\\circ)}{\\sin(20^\\circ)} \\approx \\frac{0.5}{0.3420} \\approx 1.461 \\approx \\frac{\\sin(70^\\circ)}{\\sin(40^\\circ)}\n     \\]\n   - This confirms \\( x = 30^\\circ \\).\n\n3. **Conclusion**:\n   - The measure of \\( \\angle ACD \\) is \\( \\boxed{30^\\circ} \\).", "ground_truth_solution": "1. We begin with the given isosceles triangle \\( \\triangle ABC \\), where \\( AB = AC \\) and \\( \\angle A = 80^\\circ \\).\n2. The angles at \\( B \\) and \\( C \\) can be calculated using:\n   \\[\n   \\angle B = \\angle C = \\frac{180^\\circ - \\angle A}{