s n = 118\n    \\]\n\n2. Notice that both 26019 and 649 are divisible by 59 (since \\( 26019 = 59 \\times 441 \\) and \\( 649 = 59 \\times 11 \\)). We can simplify this equation by dividing every term by 59:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{26019 \\times m - 649 \\times n}{59} = \\frac{118}{59}\n    \\]\n    Simplifying gives:\n    \\[\n    441m - 11n = 2\n    \\]\n\n3. We need to solve the linear Diophantine equation:\n    \\[\n    441m - 11n = 2\n    \\]\n\n4. To find integer solutions for \\( m \\) and \\( n \\), rewrite \\( n \\):\n    \\[\n    441m - 11n = 2 \\implies 11n = 441m - 2\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    n = \\frac{441m - 2}{11}\n    \\]\n\n5. For \\( n \\) to be an integer, \\( 441m - 2 \\) must be divisible by 11. Express \\( 441m - 2 \\) in a form suitable for divisibility analysis:\n    \\[\n    441m - 2 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{11}\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    441 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{11} \\quad (\\text{since } 441 = 40 \\times 11 + 1)\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    m \\equiv 2 \\pmod{11}\n    \\]\n\nThus, \\( m = 11k + 2 \\) for some integer \\( k \\).\n\n6. Substituting \\( m = 11k + 2 \\) into the equation \\( n = \\frac{441m - 2}{11} \\):\n    \\[\n    n = \\frac{441(11k + 2) - 2}{11} \n    \\]\n    \\[\n    = \\frac{441 \\times 11k + 882 - 2}{11} \n    \\]\n    \\[\n    = 441k + \\frac{880}{11} \n    \\]\n    \\[\n    = 441k + 80\n    \\]\n\nTherefore, given a particular integer \\( k \\), the values of \\( m \\) and \\( n \\) are:\n    \\[\n    m = 11k + 2\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    n = 441k + 80\n    \\]\n\nFor the smallest natural number solution, set \\( k = 0 \\):\n    \\[\n    m = 11 \\times 0 + 2 = 2\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    n = 441 \\times 0 + 80 = 80\n    \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{m = 2, \\; n = 80}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "The year 2013 has begun, and Xiao Ming's elder brother remarked: \"This is the first time in my life that I am going to experience a year without repeated digits.\" It is kn